                <head>231. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>On March 25 in Rome, representatives of Belgium, France, the Federal Republic of
                    Germany, Italy, Luxembourg, and the Netherlands signed the treaties establishing
                    a European Economic Community (Common Market) and a European Atomic Energy
                    Community (EURATOM). The treaties, which had been drafted in final form by the
                    Intergovernmental Committee in Brussels on March 9, would enter into force after
                    they had been ratified by all six countries. The text of the treaties is printed
                    in <hi rendition="#italic">American Foreign Policy: Current Documents,
                    1957</hi>, pages 426–573.</p>
            </div>
